     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, and I won't enter it as written. A confirmation alone doesn't change the safety problem. 8,500 mg three times daily is 25,500 mg per day, about 10 times the maximum daily dose. That is in the range of a toxic overdose, and it could cause severe lactic acidosis even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. You also haven't given a clinical reason for it.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Give me the dose you want, such as 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum) or 500 mg PO three times daily. I'll enter it right away.\n- **Check with your resident, attending, or pharmacist.** If the 8,500 mg figure came from someone else, please verify it with them directly. It may be a transcription or unit error, for example 850 mg.\n- **Escalate if this is an ingestion.** If the patient has actually taken a large amount of metformin, that is a toxicology problem, not a medication order. Contact poison control or toxicology and your team now.\n\nIf a supervising physician has a documented reason for this dose, they can enter it or work with pharmacy to do so."
      }
     ],
